dns-parallel-lookups: The dns client tools (dnsip and friends) ask every
name and every @server one after another, because dnsopt_run calls the
lookup function in a plain loop. With several servers the slowest one holds
up all the others, and a dead server costs its full timeout each time.

Doing the lookups side by side needs changes in the library, which is why it
was left out. dns_resolve and dns_ip4 use the single global dns_resolve_tx
and a blocking loop around iopause, and dns_resolvconfip returns one
process-wide server list that dnsopt switches through the environment. The
plan is this:

  - Give dns_ip4, dns_ip6, dns_mx, dns_txt and dns_name a variant that takes
    its own struct dns_transmit and server list, with a start function and a
    step function that finishes without blocking, like dns_transmit_start,
    dns_transmit_io and dns_transmit_get already do.
  - Let dnsopt_run start one transmit per name and server pair, collect the
    iopause_fd entries into one array, call iopause once and step each
    transmit that is ready.
  - Print the results in command line order, not in arrival order, so the
    server prefix lines stay grouped per name.

The pieces to touch are lib/dns/dns_resolve.c, dns_ip4.c, dns_ip6.c,
dns_mx.c, dns_txt.c, dns_name.c and dnsopt.c.

terminal-windows-untested: The terminal module is only compile checked
for a native Windows console, with mingw. Nothing of it has run there yet,
neither the output nor terminal_getkey.

It needs a run on a real console that checks text with UTF-8, colors and
styles, the erase and scroll calls, the alternate screen, cursor save and
restore, and the keys: arrows, page keys, function keys with modifiers, and
the resize event. least itself does not build for Windows yet, so a small
test program that calls the terminal functions is the quickest way.

On POSIX a size change is still signalled by SIGWINCH and handled by the
program. A terminal_resize hook that installs the handler and reports it as
TERMINAL_KEY_RESIZE through a self-pipe would make both systems look the
same.


- genmakefile-compound-literals: The compiler setup in src/genmakefile/
  compiler.c builds its flag lists with a series of var_push calls. The
  predefined macros of a compiler in project.c, bcc55_defs, show a tidier
  way: a plain list of strings that the code walks over.

  Where such a list is used in only one place, it need not be a named
  variable at all. A compound literal at the point of use, for example a
  (const char* const[]){...} passed to a small helper that pushes every
  entry, keeps the data next to the code that needs it. Rework the
  per-compiler branches of set_compiler_type this way, and let the table of
  bcc55 macros move into the same place. Not started; the move of the
  compiler code into compiler.c was done as it was.

- genmakefile-scan-diagnostics: The preprocessor scan of the sources
  prints what lib/cpp reports for a unit: warnings about #warning lines,
  "extra token" at an #endif, and errors such as "expected ')'" at lines
  that are plain code. A run over src/ prints several hundred such lines,
  which hides what -v tells.

  In scan_only mode the diagnostics of the preprocessor should be collected
  and shown only when -v is given twice, with a count at -v. The errors on
  plain code lines (src/genmakefile/project.c:254 is one) need a look of
  their own, since a scan should not parse code at all.

brotli-bcc55-c99:
brotli builds with bcc102 (clang, C99) but not with Borland C++ 5.5 or
5.6, whose compilers are C89. The generated makefile is fine; what is
left for them is a handful of declarations after statements and a for
loop that declares its counter, plus a stdint.h (third_party/compat/bcc55
has one).

Places found:
  c/dec/decode.c lines 491-493 and 2092
  c/enc/matching_tag_mask.h line 39
  c/enc/encode.c line 248

Patch the vendored tree or carry the changes as a patch file; decide which,
or leave brotli to bcc102.

genmakefile-skip-unbuildable-units:
A unit whose preprocessor scan records a missing include, unistd.h or
sys/socket.h for a Windows compiler, cannot be built for that compiler.
Today the compile fails and make goes on with -i. The Unix-only programs
(ftp, dnsutils, serial, tcp) are the bulk of the 224 error lines left with
bcc102. genmakefile could leave such a unit out and say so with -v.

Portable files that lack a guard must still be fixed in the source, so the
skip should list what it left out.

genmakefile-pragma-comment-lib:
Win32 compilers name import libraries from the source with a pragma
comment(lib, "shlwapi.lib"). genmakefile could read these during the
preprocessor scan and add them to the libraries of the program, and show
them with -v when porting a foreign project. Details in
.ai-context/genmakefile-idea-pragma-comment-lib.md.

- gemini-json-array-stream:
  Without alt=sse the streamGenerateContent method of Gemini answers with
  one JSON array that is written piece by piece, an element at a time. It
  is neither SSE nor NDJSON, so cgemini asks for alt=sse and cannot read
  that form.

  A reader that hands out the elements of a top level array as they
  complete would serve it. It could live next to ndjson_feed in the json
  module.

- http-keepalive-streams:
  The streaming clients open a connection for every request and send
  Connection: close. A conversation of many turns pays the TCP and TLS
  handshake each time. http_recv knows where a response ends, so a reuse
  of the connection for the next request is possible when keepalive is set.

- http-tls-in-body-flush:
  http_body_flush writes the queued body with http_socket_write, which
  retries a TLS write after a want-write error. This was tested with plain
  sockets only, against a local server. A run against a real TLS server
  with a body larger than the socket buffer is still to be done.

- one-ordered-map:
  The tree has hashmap, hmap, set, cbmap, cb and bmap, and map.h picks one of
  two at compile time with a define at the top of each file. Four of them do
  the same job. A set whose buckets carry a value is an ordered map; set
  already has the growth, the insertion order and the safe failure handling.

  Replace hmap, hashmap and bmap by one type built on the set code, keep the
  MAP macros as a thin layer, and retire the define. Check the users first:
  json, xml, ini, genmakefile and a dozen tools go through map.h.

- bounded-binary-views:
  The format modules take a base pointer and no size. elf_check and pe_check
  validate a whole image up front, which is a stopgap. A view type with a
  pointer and a length, checked on every access, would make the accessors
  safe by themselves and remove the need to remember the check.

  Do ELF and PE first, then COFF, OMF and Mach-O, and make binfmt_open the one
  entry point that sniffs the format and returns the right view.

- tests-for-the-rest:
  Sixteen modules have no test file: bmap, charbuf, coff, cpp, debug, dns,
  gpio, omf, playlist, process, sig, terminal, textbuf, tls and ucs. Others
  have far less than their size warrants: textcode, strarray, iob.

  Start with the ones that parse untrusted input or carry the program logic:
  cpp, dns packets, charbuf, playlist, textcode. A fuzz target for each
  parser would find most crashes cheaply.

- continuous-integration:
  There is no CI configuration. A job that builds with the strict warnings
  for the files that are clean, runs the unit tests under valgrind or ASan and
  runs the n and 4n timing check used in the review would stop the classes
  of defect found in the review from coming back.

- man-pages-against-headers:
  Several man pages described functions that do not exist. The http, sse,
  ndjson and set pages follow the code now. A script that compares the
  synopsis of every page with the declaration in the header would find the
  rest.

- genmakefile-implement-spec:
  doc/genmakefile-spec.md and doc/genmakefile-toolchains.md now describe the
  whole tool, including the PIC use case: project file, symbol closure source
  inference, MPLAB X and MPLAB 8 importers, the build matrix and the
  toolchain descriptors for XC8, XC16, XC32, SDCC, MCC18 and gputils.

  Only the specification exists. Implement it in the order of the roadmap
  section: project file first, then the importers, then source inference.

- unit-test-gaps:
  About 400 library functions still have no test that names them. The
  largest groups are dns, http, io, unix, terminal, tls, gpio, and the
  internals of hmap, cbmap, set, hashmap, stralloc and buffer. Network and
  OpenSSL dependent functions need a loopback fixture. About 35 placeholder
  tests are still empty; list them with grep -n "TEST(.*) {}".

